    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new step is well-placed overall \u2014 it's correctly split into its own function class, wired between `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` and `namesakeCount`, uses the `@Val Owner` + repository-by-type + in-place mutation idiom, names its method `service` consistent with `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`, and carries javadoc matching the house style. The one structural problem is duplicated ownership of the customer-code format:\n\n**Finding \u2014 separator/format knowledge duplicated instead of reusing `CustomerCode`**\n\n`CustomerCode` (line 5-15) explicitly declares itself \"The single definition of an owner's `customerCode` format\" and exists to \"co-locate construction and read-back so both ... cannot drift apart.\" The new class bypasses it:\n\n- It redeclares `private static final char SEPARATOR = '-';` (line 20 of the new file), a second definition of the exact `'-'` that `CustomerCode.SEPARATOR` (its line 18) is meant to be the sole owner of.\n- It builds the suffixed code inline with `base + SEPARATOR + n`, introducing a new `<REGION>-<HASH8>-<n>` layout that the format-owning class knows nothing about.\n\nThis is precisely the drift the `CustomerCode` javadoc warns against. The de-duplication *suffix* is a customer-code-format concern and should live in `CustomerCode` so the separator and layout stay in one place \u2014 e.g. add a `CustomerCode.withSuffix(String base, int n)` (or `deduplicated`) helper and have `DeduplicateOwnerCustomerCode` call it, dropping its local `SEPARATOR` constant. That keeps `DeduplicateOwnerCustomerCode` focused on the collision-scanning loop (its real job) and keeps format knowledge centralized, matching the pattern `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` already follows by calling `CustomerCode.format(...)`.\n\n`FIRST_SUFFIX` and the loop itself are fine to keep in the step, since they express the de-duplication policy rather than the code's format.",
